(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xiii"] = {"qs": [["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Interest rate futures prices rise when:", ["Bond yields rise", "Inflation expectations rise sharply", "Bond yields fall", "The RBI raises the repo rate"], 2, "IRF prices track bond prices, which rise when yields fall. Rate hikes and higher inflation expectations usually push yields up and IRF prices down."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Why should a broker not accept cash from clients for margins?", ["Cash is never accepted anywhere in India", "Rules require traceable banking channels", "Cash payments lower the margin needed", "Cash is safer than bank transfers"], 1, "SEBI norms require client funds to be received through banking channels from the client's own account for traceability and to prevent misuse."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Physical delivery of a commodity futures contract means that at expiry:", ["The seller delivers the commodity to the buyer", "Only price differences are paid in cash", "The contract is rolled over automatically to the next month", "The exchange buys the commodity"], 0, "In physically settled contracts, sellers deliver the actual commodity meeting quality norms at designated warehouses, and buyers pay the settlement value."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which of the following derivatives is usually traded over the counter rather than on an exchange?", ["Nifty index futures traded on the NSE", "USD/INR exchange-traded options", "A currency swap between two companies", "Gold futures on a commodity exchange"], 2, "Swaps are customised bilateral contracts, generally traded OTC. Index futures, exchange-traded currency options and commodity futures are standardised exchange products."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "When market interest rates rise, the prices of existing government bonds generally:", ["Fall", "Rise", "Stay the same", "Double"], 0, "Bond prices move inversely to yields. Existing bonds with lower coupons become less attractive when new bonds offer higher rates, so their prices fall."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Under interest rate parity, if Indian interest rates are higher than US rates, the USD/INR forward will trade at:", ["A discount to spot", "Exactly the spot rate", "Zero", "A premium to spot"], 3, "The currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward discount, so the dollar trades at a forward premium against the rupee. This offsets the interest differential."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "In a contango market, futures prices are:", ["Lower than spot prices", "Equal to spot prices at all times", "Not linked to spot prices", "Higher than spot prices"], 3, "Contango means futures trade above spot, usually reflecting carrying costs. Backwardation is the reverse, often due to supply shortages or high convenience yield."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Before trading derivatives, a client must be assessed for:", ["His preferred news channel", "Suitability and financial capacity", "His favourite sports team", "His social media followers"], 1, "Brokers must assess whether the client has adequate financial capacity and understanding, often through income proof, before enabling derivatives."], ["Introduction to Options", "A put option with strike ₹1,000 and premium ₹30 is bought. At expiry the stock is at ₹1,050. The buyer's net result per share is:", ["Gain of ₹50", "Loss of ₹30", "Loss of ₹80", "Gain of ₹20"], 1, "The put expires worthless because the stock is above the strike, so the buyer loses only the premium of ₹30."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A client notices trades in his account that he did not authorise. His first step should be to:", ["Ignore it if the trades made profit", "Wait for the year-end statement", "Complain to the broker at once", "Close his bank account"], 2, "Clients should raise unauthorised trades immediately with the broker and escalate to the exchange or SCORES if unresolved. Delay can weaken the claim."], ["Introduction to Options", "Which option is out of the money?", ["A call with strike above spot", "A call with strike below spot", "A put with strike above spot", "A put with strike equal to spot"], 0, "A call is OTM when strike exceeds spot, as exercise would mean buying above market. A put with strike above spot is in the money."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Under Ind AS, hedge accounting allows a company to:", ["Match hedge and hedged-item results", "Avoid recording derivatives in books", "Show derivatives only at cost", "Postpone all losses indefinitely"], 0, "Hedge accounting aligns recognition of gains and losses on the hedging instrument with those on the hedged item, reducing artificial volatility in profit."], ["Introduction to Options", "The seller of a put option receives the premium and is obliged to:", ["Sell the underlying at the strike if exercised", "Pay the premium back at expiry", "Buy the underlying at the strike if exercised", "Deliver a call option to the buyer"], 2, "A put gives its buyer the right to sell; the put writer must buy at the strike if exercised. Call writers have the obligation to sell."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "An investor holding shares worth ₹30 lakh wants to raise market exposure temporarily without buying more shares. She can:", ["Sell index futures", "Sell her shares", "Buy index futures", "Buy put options on the index"], 2, "Buying index futures adds market exposure with only margin. Selling futures, selling shares or buying puts would reduce exposure."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "SEBI imposes position limits on derivatives mainly to:", ["Prevent concentration and manipulation", "Guarantee profits to small traders", "Increase brokerage income", "Fix the price of derivatives"], 0, "Position limits stop any single participant from dominating a contract, reducing manipulation and default risk."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "A portfolio worth ₹60 lakh has a beta of 1.5. Each index futures contract is worth ₹15 lakh. How many contracts should be sold for a full hedge?", ["6", "4", "9", "3"], 0, "Contracts = value × beta ÷ contract value = 60 × 1.5 ÷ 15 = 6. Ignoring beta gives 4, which would leave the portfolio under-hedged."], ["Introduction to Options", "An option that can be exercised only on the expiry date is called:", ["American style", "Bermudan style", "Asian style", "European style"], 3, "European options can be exercised only at expiry, which is the style used for exchange-traded options in India. American options can be exercised any time before expiry."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Extreme loss margin is charged in addition to initial margin to cover:", ["Losses beyond normal risk estimates", "Brokerage and stamp duty", "Losses only on expiry day", "Taxes payable on profits"], 0, "Extreme loss margin covers losses in situations beyond those captured by the initial margin model, adding a further buffer for the clearing corporation."], ["Introduction to Options", "A call option's delta of 0.5 means that for a ₹1 rise in the underlying, the option price rises by about:", ["₹1.00", "₹5.00", "₹0.05", "₹0.50"], 3, "Delta measures the change in option price for a one-unit change in the underlying. A delta of 0.5 implies about ₹0.50 change, typical for at-the-money calls."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "Spot gold is ₹72,000 per 10 g and the near-month futures price is ₹72,900. The basis (spot minus futures) is:", ["+₹900", "₹0", "−₹900", "−₹72,900"], 2, "Basis = spot − futures = 72,000 − 72,900 = −₹900. A negative basis indicates futures trading above spot, as in a contango market."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Do exchange-traded currency futures attract Securities Transaction Tax?", ["Yes, on the buyer", "No", "Yes, on both parties", "Only on expiry day"], 1, "STT applies to securities transactions such as equity and equity derivatives. Currency derivatives are not covered by STT."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Commodity derivatives on Indian exchanges are now regulated by:", ["The Forward Markets Commission", "SEBI", "The RBI", "The Ministry of Consumer Affairs"], 1, "The Forward Markets Commission merged into SEBI in 2015, and SEBI now regulates commodity derivatives exchanges and brokers along with securities markets."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "A trader buys near-month gold futures and sells far-month gold futures. This is a:", ["Inter-commodity spread", "Protective put", "Covered call", "Calendar spread"], 3, "A calendar spread takes opposite positions in the same commodity across different expiries, profiting from changes in their price difference."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Which law governs foreign exchange transactions, including the use of currency derivatives by residents?", ["Companies Act, 2013", "Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999", "Insurance Laws (Amendment) Act, 2015 and the Insurance Act", "Indian Partnership Act, 1932"], 1, "FEMA regulates foreign exchange dealings; RBI frames rules under it for currency derivatives. The other laws concern companies, insurance and partnerships."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Exchange-traded currency derivatives in India are jointly governed by:", ["SEBI and the RBI", "IRDAI and PFRDA", "Only the Ministry of Commerce", "Only the stock exchanges"], 0, "Currency derivatives fall under FEMA, administered by the RBI, while exchanges and brokers are regulated by SEBI. Hence both regulators issue directions."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "A short straddle carries which risk?", ["Loss limited to the premium paid", "Loss only if the price stays still", "Large losses on big moves either way", "No risk, as premiums are received"], 2, "Selling a call and a put at the same strike earns premium but loses heavily if the price moves sharply in either direction."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "An investor holds a stock and sells an OTM call on it to earn income. If the stock rises sharply, she will:", ["Miss gains above the strike", "Lose more than the stock's rise", "Receive unlimited profits", "Lose the premium received"], 0, "A covered call caps upside at the strike, as she must deliver the stock if assigned. She keeps the premium but gives up further gains."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "A rice exporter sells USD forward to protect against rupee appreciation. Her role is that of a:", ["Hedger", "Speculator", "Arbitrageur", "Market maker"], 0, "She has a genuine foreign currency exposure and uses a forward to offset it, which is hedging. Speculators take positions without an underlying exposure."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Insider trading rules apply to derivatives because:", ["Derivatives are not regulated by SEBI", "Their prices depend on the underlying", "Only cash market trades can be manipulated", "Insiders are barred only from mutual funds"], 1, "Trading derivatives on unpublished price-sensitive information gives the same unfair advantage as trading shares, so insider trading regulations cover them."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A broker uses one client's credit balance to meet another client's margin shortfall. This is:", ["Allowed if both are related", "A standard netting practice", "Required by exchange rules", "Prohibited misuse of client funds"], 3, "Client funds must be used only for that client's obligations. Using them for another client is misuse and attracts strict action."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Commodity Transaction Tax (CTT) is levied on:", ["Purchase of physical agricultural goods", "Currency futures trades", "Sale of non-agricultural commodity derivatives", "Equity cash market trades"], 2, "CTT applies to taxable commodity derivatives, mainly non-agricultural contracts, on the seller. Currency derivatives do not attract STT or CTT."], ["Introduction to Options", "Which factor increases the premium of both calls and puts?", ["Lower volatility of the underlying", "Higher volatility of the underlying", "Shorter time to expiry", "A drop in market liquidity only"], 1, "Higher volatility raises the chance of large moves, benefiting option holders, so both call and put premiums rise. Shorter time to expiry reduces premiums."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "A trader expects a big move in a stock after results but is unsure of direction. Which strategy fits?", ["Short straddle", "Covered call", "Long straddle", "Bull call spread"], 2, "A long straddle (buy call and put at the same strike) profits from a large move either way. A short straddle profits only if the price stays still."], ["Introduction to Options", "A USD/INR call option has a strike of 84.00 and the spot is 84.75. Its intrinsic value is:", ["₹0.75", "Zero", "₹84.75", "₹1.50"], 0, "Call intrinsic value = spot − strike = 84.75 − 84.00 = ₹0.75 per dollar. It is positive because the call is in the money."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "An exporter expecting USD inflows buys a USD/INR put option. This protects him against:", ["A rise in USD/INR", "A fall in USD/INR", "Rising interest rates", "Higher brokerage"], 1, "A put gains if USD/INR falls, offsetting lower rupee value of his dollar receipts, while letting him benefit if the rupee weakens."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Convenience yield in commodity futures refers to:", ["The benefit of holding the physical good", "Brokerage charged by the exchange", "Interest earned on margin money kept with the broker", "Profit made by a speculator"], 0, "Convenience yield is the benefit to users of holding physical stock, such as avoiding production disruption. It can push futures prices below full cost of carry."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "A trader buys one ₹100 call, sells two ₹110 calls and buys one ₹120 call. His maximum profit occurs if the stock ends at:", ["₹100", "₹110", "₹120", "₹130"], 1, "This is a long call butterfly, which peaks at the middle strike of ₹110 and has limited loss on either side."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Brokers must give clients a risk disclosure document mainly to:", ["Advertise new products", "Collect personal data for marketing", "Guarantee returns", "Explain the risks of trading"], 3, "The risk disclosure document informs clients of leverage, potential losses and other risks before trading, enabling informed decisions."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "Which strategy is suitable for an investor who is mildly bullish and wants to reduce the cost of buying a call?", ["Long put", "Bear put spread", "Short call alone", "Bull call spread"], 3, "A bull call spread buys a lower-strike call and sells a higher-strike call, reducing cost while capping upside. Puts and bear spreads are bearish."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "A trader sells a ₹500 call at ₹14 and buys a ₹520 call at ₹5, same expiry. What is his maximum profit per share?", ["₹20", "₹11", "₹19", "₹9"], 3, "This bear call spread earns a net credit of 14 − 5 = ₹9, the maximum profit if the stock ends below ₹500. Maximum loss is 20 − 9 = ₹11."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "If the rupee depreciates against the dollar, the USD/INR rate will:", ["Fall", "Remain unchanged", "Become negative", "Rise"], 3, "Depreciation means more rupees are needed to buy one dollar, so USD/INR increases, for example from 84 to 85."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Who frames the regulations on margins, position limits and contract design for exchange-traded derivatives?", ["Each client individually", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "Credit rating agencies", "SEBI, with exchanges implementing them"], 3, "SEBI sets the framework, and exchanges and clearing corporations implement contract specifications, margins and limits within it."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A client's open position exceeds the client-level position limit. What is the likely consequence?", ["The exchange increases the limit for him", "His broker's licence is automatically cancelled", "Penalties and required reduction", "Nothing, limits are only advisory"], 2, "Exchanges levy penalties and require positions above limits to be reduced. Limits are binding and monitored to prevent concentration."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Novation in exchange-traded derivatives means:", ["The exchange cancels all trades at day end", "Brokers trade only with their own clients", "The clearing corporation is counterparty to all", "SEBI approves each trade individually"], 2, "Through novation, the clearing corporation interposes itself as buyer to every seller and seller to every buyer, guaranteeing settlement."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Stamp duty on derivatives trades in India is collected:", ["Through exchanges, from the buyer", "By SEBI from the seller", "By the RBI from banks", "By the depository from issuers"], 0, "Stamp duty on securities transactions, including derivatives, is collected centrally by exchanges or clearing corporations from the buyer and passed to states."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "Basis risk in a hedge arises because:", ["The exchange may close permanently", "Margins are not required in exchange-traded futures", "Spot and futures may not move identically", "Futures prices never change"], 2, "If the hedged asset and the futures contract do not move in exact step, the hedge is imperfect. This basis risk remains even with the correct number of contracts."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A broker's employee advises a first-time client to put all his savings into commodity futures because 'prices only go up'. This is:", ["Mis-selling and a code breach", "Good advice for a new investor", "Permitted if the client agrees", "Required to increase market liquidity"], 0, "Recommending highly leveraged products without suitability assessment and with false claims is mis-selling and breaches the code of conduct."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "EUR/USD is 1.08 and USD/INR is 84.00. What is the implied EUR/INR rate?", ["90.72", "77.78", "85.08", "83.00"], 0, "EUR/INR = EUR/USD × USD/INR = 1.08 × 84.00 = 90.72. Dividing instead gives 77.78, which is the wrong direction for this cross."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which risk is a key concern in OTC derivatives but much lower in exchange-traded ones?", ["Price risk on the underlying", "Changes in interest rates", "Counterparty default risk", "Volatility of the underlying"], 2, "Exchange trades are guaranteed by a clearing corporation through novation and margins. OTC deals rely on the counterparty's creditworthiness, though central clearing has reduced this for some products."], ["Option Trading Strategies", "A long strangle differs from a long straddle because the strangle:", ["Uses different out-of-the-money strikes", "Uses the same strike for call and put", "Involves selling both options", "Has unlimited risk"], 0, "A long strangle buys an OTM call and OTM put, making it cheaper than a straddle but needing a bigger move to profit. Both have limited risk."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "Which platform offers online conciliation and arbitration for disputes between investors and market intermediaries?", ["The Insurance Ombudsman", "The Consumer Affairs helpline", "SEBI's ODR portal", "The Income Tax portal"], 2, "SEBI's Online Dispute Resolution platform handles conciliation and arbitration of securities market disputes, including derivatives, after initial complaint routes."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "A USD/INR futures contract has a lot size of USD 1,000. A trader buys one lot at 84.50 and sells it at 84.80. Ignoring costs, his profit is:", ["₹30", "₹300", "₹3,000", "₹0.30"], 1, "Profit = (84.80 − 84.50) × 1,000 = ₹300. The rate rose 30 paise per dollar on a 1,000-dollar contract."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "A trader buys crude oil futures and sells natural gas futures expecting their price relationship to change. This is:", ["An inter-commodity spread", "A calendar spread", "A cash-and-carry arbitrage", "A naked short position"], 0, "Positions in two related commodities aim to profit from changes in their relative prices. Calendar spreads use the same commodity at different expiries."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "An entity wants to act as a clearing member in the derivatives segment. It must:", ["Only open a bank account", "Get approval from the RBI alone", "Meet net worth norms and be registered", "Only be a listed company"], 2, "Clearing members must meet SEBI and clearing corporation criteria, including net worth and deposits, and be registered. Listing or a bank account alone is insufficient."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Why do clearing corporations conduct daily mark-to-market settlement of futures?", ["To generate extra revenue for members", "To set the next day's opening price", "To decide which stocks enter the index", "To stop losses piling up"], 3, "Settling gains and losses daily keeps exposures small and lowers default risk. MTM is not a revenue tool or a price-setting mechanism."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "Spreading false rumours to move the price of a futures contract for personal gain is:", ["A permitted trading strategy", "Allowed if done on social media", "Only an ethical concern with no penalty", "A fraudulent practice punishable by SEBI"], 3, "Spreading false information to influence prices is prohibited under SEBI's rules against fraudulent and unfair trade practices and can attract penalties."], ["Legal and Regulatory Environment", "A commodity broker must register with:", ["FMC", "RBI", "SEBI", "NABARD"], 2, "After the FMC merged with SEBI in 2015, commodity derivatives brokers are registered and regulated by SEBI like other stock brokers."], ["Sales Practices, Code of Conduct & Investor Protection", "A broker shares a client's trading details with another client without consent. This breaches:", ["Confidentiality obligations", "Position limits", "Price band rules", "Margin rules"], 0, "Brokers must keep client information confidential except where law or consent permits disclosure. Sharing with other clients is a breach."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "An option differs from a futures contract because the option buyer:", ["Has an obligation to buy or sell at expiry", "Pays no money at the start", "Faces unlimited losses", "Has a right but not an obligation"], 3, "An option buyer pays a premium for the right to exercise, with no obligation. Futures bind both parties, and option buyers' losses are limited to the premium."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Premium received by an option writer on an open position is initially shown as:", ["Income immediately", "A liability until expiry or exercise", "Share capital", "A fixed asset"], 1, "The premium received is held as a liability because the writer still has an obligation. It is recognised as income when the option expires or is closed."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "A bond portfolio manager expects interest rates to rise. To hedge, he should:", ["Buy interest rate futures", "Buy more long-term bonds", "Do nothing, as bonds rise with rates", "Sell interest rate futures"], 3, "Rising rates lower bond and IRF prices, so a short IRF position gains and offsets portfolio losses. Buying futures or bonds would increase exposure."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "In the quote USD/INR = 84.00, the base currency is:", ["INR", "USD", "Both equally", "Neither, it is a cross rate"], 1, "In a pair quoted as A/B, A is the base currency and the price shows how many units of B buy one unit of A. Here one USD costs 84 rupees."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "If a client fails to pay a margin call, the broker may:", ["Ask the exchange to waive the margin", "Close out the client's position", "Transfer the loss to another client", "Extend the deadline indefinitely"], 1, "Brokers can square off positions of clients who fail to meet margin calls, to limit losses. Using other clients' funds is prohibited."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "The underlying of an interest rate futures contract in India is typically:", ["A government security or interest rate", "A basket of listed equity shares in the Nifty", "A tonne of steel", "A foreign currency pair"], 0, "Interest rate futures are based on government bonds or reference rates, letting users hedge interest rate risk. Equity, commodity and currency futures have other underlyings."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "Who conducts auctions of government dated securities and treasury bills on behalf of the Government of India?", ["SEBI", "Reserve Bank of India", "NSE", "Clearing Corporation of India"], 1, "The RBI manages public debt and conducts primary auctions of G-secs and T-bills. CCIL clears and settles trades in these securities."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "A futures position worth ₹12 lakh requires an initial margin of 10%. How much initial margin is needed?", ["₹1,20,000", "₹12,000", "₹10,00,000", "₹1,00,000"], 0, "Initial margin = 10% × ₹12,00,000 = ₹1,20,000. ₹12,000 is 1% of the value, which misplaces the decimal."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "A physical commodity market differs from the equity market because commodity prices are strongly affected by:", ["Company quarterly earnings", "Dividend announcements", "Share buyback decisions", "Weather, harvests and global supply"], 3, "Agricultural and other commodity prices depend on supply factors like weather, crop size, inventories and global demand. Earnings and dividends drive equity prices."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "An importer who must pay USD in three months fears a rising USD/INR. He should:", ["Sell USD/INR futures", "Buy USD/INR futures", "Buy INR call options from a friend", "Wait and buy dollars later"], 1, "Buying USD/INR futures locks in the rupee cost of dollars. If the rupee weakens, futures gains offset the higher cost of buying dollars."], ["Strategies Using Futures", "An exporter hedges USD receivables by selling USD/INR futures. If the rupee weakens sharply instead, the exporter will:", ["Gain on both the futures and the dollar receivables", "Lose on futures but gain on receivables", "Lose on both futures and receivables", "Be unaffected on both positions"], 1, "A weaker rupee raises the rupee value of receivables but causes losses on short futures. The hedge locks in a rate, giving up gains from favourable moves."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "Exchange-traded currency futures in India are settled:", ["By physical delivery of US dollars", "In cash in Indian rupees", "In gold at the exchange vault", "Through the RBI's forex reserves"], 1, "Currency futures on Indian exchanges are cash settled in rupees; no foreign currency is delivered. Settlement is based on the FBIL reference rate at expiry."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Which feature makes derivatives useful for transferring risk?", ["They remove risk from the economy entirely", "Risk passes to those willing to bear it", "They guarantee profits to both parties", "They can be used only by banks"], 1, "Derivatives move price risk from hedgers to speculators or other hedgers with opposite exposures. Total risk in the economy is not eliminated."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "Losses from F&O trading treated as non-speculative business can be carried forward for up to:", ["Two years", "Four years", "Indefinitely", "Eight years"], 3, "Non-speculative business losses can be carried forward for eight assessment years to be set off against business income, provided the return is filed on time."], ["Introduction to Options", "Theta of a long option position measures:", ["Change in value as volatility rises", "Change in value as interest rates rise", "Loss in value as time passes", "Change in delta as price moves"], 2, "Theta is time decay, the fall in an option's value as expiry approaches, other factors constant. Vega relates to volatility and rho to interest rates."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "USD/INR spot is 84.00 and the six-month forward is 85.26. The annualised forward premium is about:", ["1.5%", "6.0%", "3.0%", "1.26%"], 2, "Premium = (85.26 − 84.00) ÷ 84.00 = 1.5% for six months; annualised = 1.5% × 2 = 3.0%. 1.5% is the unannualised figure."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "For an individual, income from trading currency derivatives on a recognised exchange is generally treated as:", ["Speculative income", "Exempt income", "Long-term capital gain", "Non-speculative business income"], 3, "Eligible derivatives traded on recognised exchanges, including currency derivatives, are excluded from speculative transactions and treated as non-speculative business income."], ["Introduction to the Underlying Markets", "Which factor is most likely to strengthen the rupee against the dollar?", ["A sharp rise in India's oil import bill", "Large outflows by foreign portfolio investors", "Large foreign capital inflows into India", "Higher inflation in India than in the US"], 2, "Capital inflows increase demand for rupees, supporting its value. Higher imports, outflows and higher relative inflation tend to weaken the rupee."], ["Basics of Derivatives", "Arbitrage in derivatives markets helps by:", ["Increasing price differences between markets", "Removing the need for any regulation", "Guaranteeing that prices always rise", "Keeping related prices in line"], 3, "Arbitrageurs exploit price gaps between related markets; their trades push prices back into alignment, improving market efficiency."], ["Trading, Clearing, Settlement & Risk Management", "In the derivatives segment, a self-clearing member clears and settles:", ["Trades of all other members", "Only trades in the cash segment", "Trades of foreign exchanges", "Only its own and its clients' trades"], 3, "A self-clearing member clears its own proprietary and client trades. A professional or general clearing member can clear trades of other trading members."], ["Accounting and Taxation", "At year-end, an open futures position with an unrealised loss is accounted for by:", ["Ignoring it until the position is closed", "Adding it to share capital", "Recognising the loss in the accounts", "Treating it as a gain"], 2, "Accounting standards require derivatives to be measured at fair value, so MTM losses on open positions are recognised. Prudence does not allow ignoring them."], ["Introduction to Forwards and Futures", "Why must a futures trader maintain a margin account?", ["To earn guaranteed interest", "To cover daily losses", "To pay dividends on the underlying", "To pay the full contract value at expiry"], 1, "Initial margin and daily mark-to-market ensure losses are settled every day, protecting the clearing corporation. It is not a savings or dividend account."]]};
